# A program that reads its own command line two ways, and the only one in this
# repository that calls `args_checked`.
#
# Corpus and regression material (CLAUDE.md §9), and it closes a stated debt
# rather than a gap somebody noticed. `docs/measurements/010-spec-budget-ledger.md`
# row **3541** bought `args_checked`'s clause with a registered prediction, and
# that prediction was scored **FALSE** at M-separate-compilation close for one
# reason: no file in `examples/` or `tests/golden/` used the built-in, so the
# instrument the payment named had nothing to measure. The clause went back to
# `docs/work/DECIDE.md` to be re-argued under the removal branch.
#
# **And the reason the prediction gave for being unscoreable was half wrong.**
# It said `suite_run` has no way to pass argv to a golden — true — and the
# corpus suite has had one all along: `main.args`, one argument per line, read
# by `suite_corpus.program_arguments` and handed to the program behind the `--`
# that `heroes run` requires. This directory has one.
#
# WHAT THE TWO BUILT-INS ARE FOR, from `spec:186-190`:
#
#   `args() -> [str]`          the arguments after the program name; one that
#                              is not UTF-8 **aborts**
#   `args_checked() -> [str?]` the same list, which does not
#
# So `args_checked` exists for exactly one situation: a command line a user
# typed, or a shell expanded, that holds bytes no `str` can carry. A file name
# on a filesystem that never promised UTF-8 is the ordinary case.
#
# **WHAT THIS PROGRAM CANNOT DO, said rather than implied**: it cannot pass a
# non-UTF-8 argument, because `main.args` is read as a `str` and a `str` is
# UTF-8 by definition (`spec:56`). So every argument here comes back `ok`, and
# what the program pins is the SHAPE — the arity, the order, the `T?` wrapper,
# and that the two built-ins agree when nothing is malformed. The abort path
# belongs to a harness that can write raw bytes into an `execve`, which is not
# this one, and naming that limit is what keeps the next reader from thinking
# this case covers it.

function main()
    given = args()
    checked = args_checked()

    # The two agree on how many, always: `args_checked` does not drop the
    # argument it cannot decode, it reports it.
    print(given.len())
    print(checked.len() == given.len())

    # Every one of ours decodes, so every entry is an `ok` carrying the same
    # text `args()` gave.
    same: bool @ true
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < given.len()
        one = checked[i]

        if one.is_err() || one.must() != given[i]
            same @ false
        i @ i + 1

    print(same)

    # And the arguments themselves, so the expected output pins the order
    # rather than only the count. An argument holding a space survives,
    # which is `hero_run_arg`'s doing and not this program's.
    print(join(given, " | "))

    # A `T?` read the three ways the spec gives, on a value that is present.
    print(checked[0].must())
    print(checked[0].default("nothing"))
    print(checked[0].is_err())

test "the two built-ins agree on arity"
    assert args().len() == args_checked().len()

test "an index past the end is a failure, not an abort"
    # `args_checked` answers `[str?]`, so the array bound is still an array
    # bound: reading past it aborts, which is why this asks `len` instead.
    # What the case pins is the shape the spec promises — a list whose
    # elements are fallible, not a fallible list.
    checked = args_checked()
    assert checked.len() >= 0

    if checked.len() > 0
        assert !checked[0].is_err()

test "every argument this program is given decodes"
    # True of this corpus entry by construction, since `main.args` is a text
    # file. The case exists so that the day a harness can hand a program raw
    # bytes, the assertion that has to change is written down and findable.
    for one in args_checked()
        assert !one.is_err()
